            <ArticleTitle>Circulating and exhaled vascular endothelial growth factor in asthmatic pregnancy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>648-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/1354750X.2012.713004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) plays a role in asthma and pathological pregnancies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This is the first study assessing plasma and exhaled breath condensate VEGF levels in asthmatic pregnancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-one asthmatic pregnant, 29 asthmatic nonpregnant, 28 healthy pregnant and 22 healthy nonpregnant women were enrolled. Plasma was collected in all subjects, EBC in 57 volunteers for VEGF measurements.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Plasma VEGF decreased in both pregnant groups (p &lt; 0.01), without any differences between the asthmatic and the respective nonasthmatic groups (p &gt; 0.05). VEGF was undetectable in EBC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Concomitant asthma does not affect plasma VEGF during pregnancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
